lesson coding-colour-by-code v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=3 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Colour by code"
  goal "Colour a picture on squared paper from a program that says each row's colours."

  look {
    scene 27x14 {
      pixels p cols=7 rows=6 code=["1 white 2 red 1 white 2 red 1 white", "7 red", "7 red", "1 white 5 red 1 white", "2 white 3 red 2 white", "3 white 1 red 3 white"] at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      A picture on a screen is made of tiny squares, and a program can say what colour each one is.
      Each line of this code is one row, read from the left: one white, two red, one white. Colour
      the squares row by row and a picture appears.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice coding.pixel-missing-number count=1 seed=4
    show coding.pixel-row r=3 easy-r=2 hard-r=4
    practice coding.pixel-count count=2 seed=2
    practice coding.pixel-white count=2 seed=5
    practice coding.pixel-most-red count=2 seed=3
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show coding.pixel-spoilt-row r=2 k=0 easy-r=1 hard-r=4
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.pixel-ways n=6 s=2 easy-n=5 easy-s=3 hard-n=7 hard-s=2
  }
  remember {
    say "One line of code is one row of squares, from the left."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Squared paper and crayons are all this needs. Afterwards, the child can make their own picture
    and write its code for you to colour, which is the same idea the other way round. A computer
    stores a photograph in just this way, with a number for each square's colour. Some questions
    hide the picture, so the code has to be read rather than the squares counted, and one shows the
    picture and asks for the code. In the two-star question a picture that matches on both sides has
    the same number of white squares at each end of every line. In the three-star question, sliding
    the red along one square at a time finds every row.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the rows asked about come first in the code, the codes paint fewer squares, and there are three ways to place the red squares."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the row asked about is the last one, the codes paint more squares, and there are six ways to place the red squares."
  }
}
